    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27; printed page 5; Section 1.2, first paragraph",
      "anchor": "“the cell average of q, which is this integral divided by the volume of the cell”",
      "observation": "The selected passage defines the cell average by dividing the integral of q over a grid cell by that cell's volume."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27; printed page 5; Section 1.2, first paragraph",
      "anchor": "“we break the domain into grid cells”",
      "observation": "The cells are spatial pieces of the computational domain. Finite volume methods approximate cell integrals or cell averages."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27; printed page 5; Section 1.2, first paragraph",
      "anchor": "“based on the integral form (1.10)” and “modified in each time step by the flux through the edges”",
      "observation": "The definition appears in a conservation-law method whose approximate cell values evolve through cell-boundary fluxes."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26; printed page 4; Section 1.1.1, equation (1.10)",
      "anchor": "d/dt ∫ from x₁ to x₂ q(x,t) dx = f(q(x₁,t)) − f(q(x₂,t))",
      "observation": "The referenced integral form uses spatial integration with dx at a fixed time, with the time derivative of the integral determined by endpoint fluxes."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26; printed page 4; Section 1.1.1, paragraph following (1.10)",
      "anchor": "“Each component of q measures the density of some conserved quantity”",
      "observation": "The integral represents the total amount of each conserved quantity in the spatial region."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 23; printed page 1; paragraph containing (1.1)",
      "anchor": "“q : ℝ × ℝ → ℝᵐ” and “time as well as one or more spatial variables”",
      "observation": "The introductory one-dimensional setting uses a time-dependent, potentially vector-valued unknown q."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27; printed page 5; continuation of Section 1.1.2",
      "anchor": "“At a discontinuity in q” and “the integral conservation law (1.10) ... does continue to hold”",
      "observation": "The surrounding discussion explicitly includes discontinuous solutions and motivates using the integral formulation."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27; printed page 5; Section 1.2.1, paragraph following (1.11)",
      "anchor": "“If Qᵢ₋₁ and Qᵢ are the cell averages in two neighboring grid cells”",
      "observation": "Neighboring cell averages supply the left and right states for a Riemann problem used to compute numerical fluxes."
    }
  ],
  "statement": {
    "binders": [
      "q is the solution field being averaged; the inherited one-dimensional presentation permits q : ℝ × ℝ → ℝᵐ, with spatial argument x and temporal argument t [printed p. 1, paragraph containing (1.1)].",
      "A grid cell is a spatial piece of the domain; the definition applies to each such cell [printed p. 5, Section 1.2, “over each grid cell”].",
      "Time is held fixed when forming a spatial cell integral; this is inherited from q(x,t) integrated against dx in (1.10), rather than explicitly rebound in the defining sentence [printed p. 4, (1.10); printed p. 5, Section 1.2]."
    ],
    "hypotheses": [
      "The domain is broken into grid cells in the finite volume description [printed p. 5, Section 1.2, “we break the domain into grid cells”].",
      "The defining sentence presupposes that the relevant cell integral and division by cell volume are meaningful; it supplies no explicit integrability, measurability, or nonzero-volume hypotheses [printed p. 5, Section 1.2, cell-average defining clause]."
    ],
    "conclusions": [
      "For each grid cell, its cell average of q is the integral of q over that cell divided by the volume of the same cell [printed p. 5, Section 1.2, “this integral divided by the volume of the cell”].",
      "Finite volume methods approximate these cell integrals or cell averages, and the approximate values are modified at each time step using fluxes through cell edges [printed p. 5, Section 1.2, first paragraph].",
      "Numerical flux functions approximate the correct fluxes using the approximate cell averages as the available data [printed p. 5, Section 1.2, “based on the approximate cell averages, the only data available”]."
    ],
    "definitions_and_conventions": [
      "In analyst-introduced notation C for a cell, the definition reads average_C q(t) = (∫_C q(x,t) dx) / volume(C); the source gives this definition in prose rather than introducing C or this displayed formula [printed p. 5, Section 1.2, defining clause; printed p. 4, (1.10)].",
      "Volume denotes spatial cell volume; in the supplied one-dimensional context, the referenced integral is against dx over an interval [printed p. 4, (1.10); printed p. 5, Section 1.2, “volume of the cell”].",
      "For vector-valued q, the integral and its scalar normalization represent averages of the conserved-density components; this interpretation follows from the component-density description and the vector-valued declaration of q [printed p. 4, paragraph following (1.10); printed p. 1, paragraph containing (1.1)].",
      "The exact cell average is the quantity defined by the integral quotient; the numerical method uses approximate cell averages [printed p. 5, Section 1.2, “approximate” and “approximate cell averages”]."
    ],
    "implicit_context": [
      "The selected claim is a definition within a description of finite volume methods, rather than a numbered theorem with an explicit quantified hypothesis list [printed p. 5, Section 1.2, first paragraph].",
      "Conservation laws supply the physical interpretation of the cell integral as a total conserved amount and of boundary fluxes as its mechanism of change [printed p. 4, Section 1.1.1, (1.10) and following paragraph].",
      "Discontinuous solutions are part of the intended setting; classical differentiability of q is not a stated prerequisite for the cell-average definition [printed p. 5, continuation of Section 1.1.2 and opening of Section 1.2].",
      "The constant-coefficient linear system on printed page 1 is introduced as a simplest case; the subsequent context also includes nonlinear conservation laws, so linearity and a fixed matrix A are not inherited requirements of the cell-average definition [printed p. 1, paragraph containing (1.1); printed p. 4, opening paragraph; printed p. 5, Section 1.2]."
    ],
    "semantic_facets": [
      {
        "topic": "Spatial integration and normalization",
        "details": [
          "The numerator integrates q over the entire cell and the denominator is that same cell's volume [printed p. 5, Section 1.2, defining clause].",
          "The referenced source context uses spatial dx integration, and does not introduce an arbitrary measure parameter or weighted averaging convention [printed p. 4, (1.10); printed p. 5, Section 1.2, defining clause]."
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Temporal and vector structure",
        "details": [
          "The averaged field can depend on time, while the averaging region is spatial [printed p. 1, paragraph preceding (1.1); printed p. 4, (1.10)].",
          "The unknown can have m conserved-density components; the selected passage does not restrict its definition to scalar q [printed p. 1, paragraph following (1.1); printed p. 4, paragraph following (1.10)]."
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Numerical role",
        "details": [
          "Cell averages provide the data from which numerical fluxes are computed and cell values updated [printed p. 5, Section 1.2, first paragraph].",
          "The neighboring-cell indices i−1 and i occur in the later Riemann-problem application, rather than as required indices in the definition itself [printed p. 5, Section 1.2.1, paragraph following (1.11)]."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "undebatable_constraints": [
    "Preserve normalization by the volume of the same cell over which q is integrated [printed p. 5, Section 1.2, defining clause].",
    "Preserve spatial cell integration at a given time; the cited integral form is ∫ q(x,t) dx [printed p. 4, (1.10); printed p. 5, Section 1.2].",
    "Distinguish the exact defining integral quotient from the method's approximation to that quantity [printed p. 5, Section 1.2, first paragraph].",
    "Do not attribute an arbitrary-measure generalization, a weighted average, or a convention for zero-volume cells to this passage; none is specified in its definition or its cited integral form [printed p. 5, Section 1.2, defining clause; printed p. 4, (1.10)].",
    "Do not make smoothness or constant-coefficient linearity a source-stated requirement of this definition; its enclosing discussion treats discontinuities and conservation laws more broadly [printed pp. 4–5, Sections 1.1.1–1.2]."
  ],
  "ambiguities": [
    {
      "issue": "The admissibility conditions on cells and q are not formally stated.",
      "evidence": "Printed p. 5, Section 1.2 defines the average by an integral divided by volume without listing integrability, finite-volume, or positive-volume assumptions.",
      "impact": "Conditions making the quotient a conventional finite average may need to be supplied by a formalization, but must be identified as mathematical precisifications rather than explicit source hypotheses. The passage gives no exceptional-case convention."
    },
    {
      "issue": "The integration framework is not specified beyond spatial integration and cell volume.",
      "evidence": "Printed p. 4, (1.10) writes dx; printed p. 5, Section 1.2 refers to the integral over the cell and its volume without naming an integration theory.",
      "impact": "A concrete integration theory must be selected when formalizing. The source supports spatial volume averaging but does not itself assert a theorem for every measure."
    },
    {
      "issue": "Cell geometry and spatial dimension are not explicitly bound in the defining sentence.",
      "evidence": "Printed p. 1 introduces one or more spatial variables before specializing to one dimension; printed p. 4, (1.10) uses an interval; printed p. 5, Section 1.2 speaks generally of grid cells and volume.",
      "impact": "The interval interpretation is directly supported by the immediate context, while a fully quantified multidimensional formulation requires additional specification. Endpoint inclusion conventions are not given."
    }
  ],
  "contract_plain_english": "The selected passage defines the cell average of a solution field as its integral over a spatial grid cell divided by that cell's volume. Finite volume methods approximate these averages and update the approximate values using boundary fluxes computed from the available approximate averages [printed p. 5, Section 1.2, first paragraph]. The inherited context uses spatial dx integration at fixed time and permits vector-valued conserved densities and discontinuous solutions [printed p. 1, paragraph containing (1.1); printed p. 4, (1.10) and following paragraph; printed p. 5, continuation of Section 1.1.2]. The passage does not specify an arbitrary measure, analytic admissibility conditions, or behavior for zero-volume cells [printed p. 5, Section 1.2, defining clause]."
